[#:seon.task{:name "sci-pmap-escapes-the-arm"
             :plan "An agent's `pmap` runs outside its evaluation's arm: SCI's future addon (reference-code/sci/src/sci/addons/future.clj) implements `pmap` over the host `clojure.core/future-call`, while seon.sci.eval replaces only `future-call` with `seon.sci.kernel/future-call` (eval.clj:404). The time limit, allocation total and interrupt do not reach pmap's threads, and S4a's owned-work count cannot see them. Done when an agent's pmap threads are governed by the form's arm and counted as its owned work, proven by a pmap whose element blocks past the time limit being cut and its form's evidence frozen :unknown until exit. Read `seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm` and `docs/research/agent-platform/one-repl-path-spec-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-failure-keeps-and-classifies-every-frame-of-every-link"
             :plan "failure-value keeps `seon.error.refusal/chain` whole and the SCI stacktrace whole, and the chain walk (refusal.clj:441 walk-links -> stack-frames per link -> first-party-links/first-party-in, whose armed contract validates a malli regex [:vector [:cat :symbol ...]] per call) costs links x frames x validation. Measured on default 2026-10-07 through MCP sci: a recursive defn's StackOverflowError keeps 7,969 SCI frames (a 3-frame cycle) and costs 1,254 ms, 2,076 MB and 361,649 armed calls; throwing a 1,000-link ex-info chain costs 874 ms and 2,100 MB (JFR: walk-links 1.74 GB, first-party-links 1.61 GB, first-party-in 0.76 GB self, malli CheckDriver 0.40 GB). The reply shows 32 frames; the stored shown text is bounded (1,123 chars); the live error value and our work grow with stack depth and chain length. Fix at the producer: a repeated frame run is one frame with its repeat count, repeated links collapse with a count, and frames are classified in one unarmed pass inside the contracted chain. Coordinate with the errors spec slices (seon.error.refusal is held by lane no-print-hash). Done when the stack overflow and the 1,000-link chain each answer in under 100 ms and under 50 MB with every distinct first-party frame kept; regressions: both cases through the one path. Read `seon.sci.kernel/failure-value`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--error-replies.txt`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--jfr-large-errors-alloc.txt` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--large-values.edn`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-agent-exception-has-no-error-type",
            :plan
            "An agent's thrown ex-info, a host exception and a time-limit interrupt come back as error maps with no `:seon.error/type`: (throw (ex-info \"probe boom\" {})) answered keys without `:seon.error/type` (operation `seon.sci.kernel/failure-value`, layer :seon.sci.kernel/evaluation); (Integer/parseInt \"nope\") likewise in SCI and JVM mode; a 30 s loop answered :error {`:seon.error/message` \"time-limit\"} in the reply while the value's message is \"Ran out of time after 30009ms.\" and names no bound. Correct (errors spec): an undeclared exception is :seon.error/exception with its chain, a time-limit is its own declared variant naming the bound and the elapsed ms, and the reply shows the value's own message. Proof: three rows through support/agent-value asserting `:seon.error/type`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "interruptible-range-allocates-six-times-core-range"
             :plan "In an agent's SCI context `range` is sci.interrupt's range-seq (reference-code/sci/src/sci/interrupt.cljc:46-65, installed by seon.sci.eval's context options): an unchunked lazy-seq consing one element per step, calling the kernel's interrupt-fn per element, with no O(1) count. Measured on default 2026-10-08 through every thread's allocation counter: (count (range 5000000)) allocates 880 MB in SCI where JVM clojure.core's LongRange counts without realizing anything; (count (vec (range 5000000))) 907 MB in SCI against 147 MB in JVM mode; (into [] (range 5000000)) 1,603 MB; the five forms together took 2.7 s. Correct: chunked steps (one interrupt check per chunk, e.g. over clojure.core/range's chunks) and count/reduce that do not realize a bounded range. Done when seon.profile/measure of (count (vec (range 5000000))) from SCI allocates within 1.5 times the JVM-mode figure. Read `seon.profile/measure` and `seon.sci.kernel/arm`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "softly-held-index-nodes-count-as-memory-pressure"
             :plan "The memory gate reads Datahike's soft cache as live heap. persistent-sorted-set 0.4.137 builds every set with RefType.SOFT (Settings() default; Branch.store wraps stored children with Settings.makeReference), and HotSpot keeps a soft referent until it has gone unread for free-heap MB x SoftRefLRUPolicyMSPerMB (default 1000 ms: 21 minutes at the 70% gate under a 4 GiB cap). System/gc in enter-memory-pressure! therefore leaves every index node read in that window in the old pool. On default pid 79558 (e3b7bae3a, 2026-10-08) the full-GC heap grew from 0.6 GB to 1.5 GB in 13 minutes of root's test rounds, and in the 09:00Z dump 49,437 of 62,778 Leaf nodes (79%) were reachable only through soft references (MAT: 418 MB dominated by SoftReferences). This memory grows with database size times the indexes recently read, not with agents, and it sits on top of the configured, bounded node LRU (:seon.config.db/store-cache-size, shared per store by datahike.index.persistent-set/create-storage). The patch docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/heap-leak/soft-ref-policy.diff (deps.edn is held by lane impl-ref1) adds -XX:SoftRefLRUPolicyMSPerMB=10 to :dev and :test (about 12 s unread at the gate) with its regression. The successor fix is Datahike's PSS reference type as a store dial (:ref-type :weak), so that heap = strong live set + configured LRU; it needs the fressian root read handler to accept a ref type. Done when, with the patch applied and default restarted, a test round leaves the full-GC heap within the strong live set plus the node LRU (no soft-only Leaf majority in a dump) and a test round's timing is within 20% of the parent's. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration`, `seon.sci.kernel/enter-memory-pressure!`, `seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/heap-leak/soft-ref-policy.diff`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-memory-refusal-is-answered-four-times"
             :plan "One :ping refused by acquire-arm for memory pressure (default, 2026-10-08) returned two duplicate error maps plus two identical test-error maps (~1,500 tokens), one with a meaningless :seon.error/resolution digest pair; the proof step reported passed? true, red [] although evaluation was refused. Done when a refused evaluation answers one error map, no test-error is made for work never run, and the step reports the proofs as not run instead of passed. Read `seon.sci.kernel/memory-pressure-error` and `seon.turn/step`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-file-function-that-calls-slurp-cannot-be-redefined"
             :plan "Agent fan9b-anchor (fan-out 9b, default f54e73c83, 2026-10-09), assigned `seon.ref/a-missing-anchor-ranks-headings-by-shared-prefix`, sent a correct-looking `(defn section ...)` after `(in-ns 'seon.ref)` and was refused with \"Unable to resolve symbol: slurp\": `seon.ref/section` reads its file with `clojure.core/slurp`, which an agent's SCI context leaves unbound (`seon.sci.kernel/host-function-tools` only names `my.fs/read` in the error). So no agent can redefine a file function whose body calls `slurp`, `spit` or `file-seq`, although the compiled function does and AGENTS.md says nothing is isolated for security. The agent never reached the proof `seon.ref-test/a-missing-anchor-names-the-closest-spelled-heading-first`. Done when an agent's redefinition of `seon.ref/section` (its body unchanged) is admitted on its branch and answers as the compiled one does. Read `seon.sci.eval/build-base-ctx` and `seon.sci.kernel/failure-value`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-file-test-run-interpreted-inherits-the-runners-arm-and-overruns-its-bound"
             :plan "When `seon.test/resolve-test` interprets every file test, including root's (candidate run 4025b77445d3, 2026-10-10), `seon.sci.kernel-test/the-interpreter-resolves-the-classpath-and-loaded-namespaces` runs past its 5 s bound (5,008 ms; compiled it takes 2,845 ms, run 502fdd4ab69b) and errors with \"time-limit\" from `seon.sci.kernel/new-guard`. Its nested evaluation `@(future (loop [] (recur)))` asks for a 300 ms deadline, but the interpreted body runs under the test runner's own arm (`seon.sci.kernel/with-arm`), and the outer arm governs a nested one, as `seon.sci.kernel-test/a-nested-context-inherits-the-outer-arm` states, so the loop runs to the member's bound. Root's requests stay compiled for now (ruling, 2026-10-10). Decide whether an interpreted test body's own evaluations take their requested deadline under the runner's arm, or whether the test changes. Done when that test passes interpreted within its bound. Read `seon.sci.eval/run-tests`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-agent-namespace-ns-interns-lists-only-its-own-vars"
             :plan "Filed here because seon.sci.eval.edn is held by another lane; move to it. In agent SCI `(ns-interns 'probe.dd2)` answered about 120 `clojure.core` Vars (`ensure`, `gen-class`, `agent`, …) as interns of `probe.dd2` beside its own `request`, `measure` and `other` (default JVM pid 2041, HEAD 984789a27, 2026-10-14). Clojure's `ns-interns` holds only the namespace's own Vars. SCI's `sci.impl.namespaces/sci-ns-interns*` returns the namespace map minus `clean-ns`, so the core Vars are entries Seon installs into each agent namespace (the core refer every namespace receives, `seon.sci.eval` own-bindings and install-host-namespace!), stored where SCI reads interns. Done when `ns-interns` of an agent namespace returns exactly the Vars defined in it, with the core refers held where `ns-refers` reads them, and `seon.sci.lookup` and `agent-test-all-vars` (which read `sci-ns-interns*`) still find the namespace's tests. Proof: to write before assignment. Read `seon.sci.eval/agent-test-all-vars`, `seon.sci.eval/install-host-namespace!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/digest-and-def-defects.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "clojure-data-resolves-in-agent-sci"
             :plan "Filed here because seon.sci.eval.edn is held by another lane; move to it. In agent SCI `(clojure.data/diff a b)` answers \\\"Unable to resolve symbol: clojure.data/diff\\\" (default JVM pid 2041, HEAD 984789a27, 2026-10-14), although `clojure.data` is a loaded host namespace. `seon.sci.eval/install-required-libraries!` exposes a loaded library namespace only when a program namespace row requires, aliases or refers it, and SCI's `:namespaces` option (`seon.sci.eval/build-base-ctx`) carries only clojure.core and clojure.string beside the injected ones; a bare qualified symbol with no require therefore does not resolve, whereas the same call after `(require 'clojure.data)` should. Verify which of the two applies first, with `(require '[clojure.data :as d])` then `(d/diff {:a 1} {:a 2})` and the qualified form. Done when both the required and the fully qualified forms resolve `clojure.data/diff`, by one general mechanism (the library-namespace loader), with no per-namespace allowlist added beside it. Proof: to write before assignment. Read `seon.sci.eval/install-required-libraries!`, `seon.sci.eval/load-host-namespace` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/digest-and-def-defects.md`."}]
